    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.pairs\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.12 (!at least latest Nightly build!)\n\n### Description\n\nWhen a ghost text (e.g. from copilot) appears between the cursor and the closing pair:\n\n`\"some string content <cursor>some ghost text from copilot\"`\n\nWhen I type a closing quote `\"`, the current behaviour of just moving forward, instead of typing the closing quote.\nIn the presence of ghost text, it doesn't work.\nIt just moves the cursor forward inside the ghost text:\n\n`\"some string content s<cursor>ome ghost text from copilot\"`\n\nSo typing `\"` appears to be just moving cursor forward until it reaches the end of the ghost text, at which point it will correctly close the string. The result will be `\"some string content \"`.\n\nI think the solution is, instead of ignoring the typed character, and moving cursor forward, need to actually type it, and then `<del>` the next character. I tried this locally, and it worked. I'm going to submit a PR soon, though not sure if it does all correctly.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n1. Create separate 'nvim-repro' config directory:\n    - '~/.config/nvim-repro/' on Unix\n    - '~/AppData/Local/nvim-repro/' on Windows\n\n2. Inside 'nvim-repro' directory create a file named 'init.lua'.\n   Populate it with the following content:\n\n```lua\n    -- Clone latest 'mini.nvim' (requires Git CLI installed)\nvim.cmd('echo \"Installing `mini.nvim`\" | redraw')\nlocal mini_path = vim.fn.stdpath(\"data\") .. \"/site/pack/deps/start/mini.nvim\"\nlocal clone_cmd = { \"git\", \"clone\", \"--depth=1\", \"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim\", mini_path }\nvim.fn.system(clone_cmd)\nvim.cmd('echo \"`mini.nvim` is installed\" | redraw')\n\n-- Make sure 'mini.nvim' is available\nvim.cmd(\"packadd mini.nvim\")\nrequire(\"mini.deps\").setup()\nrequire(\"mini.pairs\").setup()\n\n-- we are going to be able to 'travel' inside ghost text.\nvim.opt.virtualedit = \"all\"\n\n-- this is just a crude way to simulate ghost text from copilot.\n-- it is so that I don't need to try to recreate the whole LSP and Copilot setup for this repro \n-- I didn't even write it myself, asked grok to do it\nlocal ns = vim.api.nvim_create_namespace(\"static_ghost\")\nlocal fixed_row, fixed_col\n\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd(\"InsertEnter\", {\n  callback = function()\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_clear_namespace(0, ns, 0, -1)\n    local row, col = unpack(vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0))\n    fixed_row, fixed_col = row - 1, col\n\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_extmark(0, ns, fixed_row, fixed_col, {\n      virt_text = { { \"this is ghost text\", \"Comment\" } },\n      virt_text_pos = \"inline\",\n      hl_mode = \"combine\",\n    })\n  end,\n})\n\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd(\"InsertLeave\", {\n  callback = function()\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_clear_namespace(0, ns, 0, -1)\n  end,\n})\n\nprint([[create a file 'test.txt' with the following content: \"text''text\". open\nit, place cursor on the closing quote, and press 'i' to enter the insert mode.\nthen type closing quote to see how ghost text behaves.\n  ]])\n```\n\n3. Run `NVIM_APPNAME=nvim-repro nvim` (i.e. execute `nvim` with `NVIM_APPNAME` environment variable set to \"nvim-repro\").\n   Wait for all dependencies to install.\n\n4. create a file 'test.txt' with the following content: \"text''text\". open\nit, place cursor on the closing quote, and press 'i' to enter the insert mode.\nthen type closing quote to see how ghost text behaves. The cursor will just move forward \nuntil it reaches the end of ghose text, only then it will start doing anything again.\n\n\n\n\n</details>\n",
